    <DataNode elementId="AT5G38710-MONOMER" textLabel="Proline dehydrogenase 2, mitochondrial" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q6NKX1" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="335" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Arabidopsis has two functional genes encoding proline dehydrogenase - |FRAME: AT3G30775 POX1| and |FRAME: AT5G38710 POX2| |CITS: [20403182]|. The two proteins have non-redundant, although partially overlapping physiological functions. |FRAME: AT3G30775-MONOMER &quot;Proline dehydrogenase 1&quot;| appears to be the dominant isoform under most conditions and in most tissues, while |FRAME: AT5G38710-MONOMER proline dehydrogenase 2| is specifically upregulated during salt stress (when |FRAME: AT3G30775 POX1| is repressed) |CITS: [20403182]|. |FRAME: AT5G38710-MONOMER proline dehydrogenase 2| is expressed in the vascular tissue and in the abscission zone of petals, sepals, stamina, pistils and siliques. It is Down-regulated by high succrose and up-regulated by |FRAME: PRO L-proline| and drought. When expressed in yeast the protein rescued the Pro metabolism defect of a &amp;Delta;put1 mutant strain |CITS: [20403182]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT5G38710-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT5G38710.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Panther" value="PTHR13914" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Pride" value="Q6NKX1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Interpro" value="IPR029041" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Pfam" value="PF01619" />
